  ├─────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mtask.commented[39m      │ A comment is added                        │ Yes    │ No                                │
  ├─────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────┼────────┼───────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mtask.mentioned[39m      │ [38;5;153m@name[39m appears in a comment or description │ Yes    │ [1mYes[0m, to the mentioned person only │
  └─────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────┴────────┴───────────────────────────────────┘

  So email is exactly two events, and both are directed at one specific person who has been personally singled out.
  That's the "narrow, high-signal" line you picked — nothing broadcast ever reaches an inbox.

  [1mThree rules govern who gets a notification for an event:[0m

  1. [1mNever notify the actor of their own action.[0m Filtered on write, not on read. This sounds obvious and is the single
     most common bug in these systems — it's why your own comment shows up as an unread notification in half the tools
     you've used.
  2. [1m[38;5;153mmuted[39m beats everything.[0m A muted subscription suppresses both in-app and email, even for a direct assignment. If you
     muted it, you meant it.
  3. [1mOtherwise, every [38;5;153mauto[39m or [38;5;153mwatching[39m subscriber gets a row.[0m

  [1mAuto-subscription triggers[0m (create a [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m row with [38;5;153mstate='auto'[39m, but never overwrite an existing [38;5;153mmuted[39m):
  creating a task, being assigned one, commenting on one, being @mentioned in one. Explicit watch/unwatch sets [38;5;153mwatching[39m
  or [38;5;153mmuted[39m and is never touched by the automatic rules afterward — a manual choice outranks inference permanently.

  [1mRead state:[0m mark-one-read, mark-all-read, and the badge is [38;5;153mCOUNT(*) WHERE read_at IS NULL[39m. The badge polls on a timer
  (every 30s, and on page load); no SSE or WebSockets in v1 — for an internal tool, polling a cheap indexed count is the
  right amount of machinery.

  [1mDeliberately out of scope for v1,[0m so you can push back now rather than be surprised: no digests, no per-user
  preference matrix (which events email whom), no [38;5;153m@here[39m-style broadcast mentions, no collapsing of rapid repeated edits
  into one event. Edits are one-per-save so storms are naturally bounded; if someone does hammer a task and it's
  annoying, collapsing consecutive same-actor [38;5;153mtask.edited[39m events inside a time window is the fix, and it's additive — we
  can do it later without touching the schema.

  Does that event set and the email line look right?

